WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:04.740
From action flick to rom com, everyone has
that one movie that, no matter how many times

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.250
they've seen it before, they just can't resist
watching again.

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:12.210
Maybe it's the awesome plot, or amazing effects,
or maybe it's just one, singular scene that

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.610
draws people back in again and again.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.140
What makes these scenes worthy of repeat viewings?

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.300
See for yourself.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.300
Rosebud

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.689
With one whispered word, Orson Welles single
handedly invented the concept of the mind-blowing

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:25.780
twist ending.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.720
"Rosebud."

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:35.520
Citizen Kane is considered by many to be the
greatest film ever made, and it holds up remarkably

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.520
well.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.660
But it's the twist at the end that keeps viewers
coming back.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.120
With his last dying breath, Charles Foster
Kane reminds us that despite the wealth and

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.020
power he has amassed, nothing can compare
to his innocent and happy childhood.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:51.020
"I don't think any word can explain a man's
life.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.830
No, I guess 'Rosebud' was just a piece in
a jigsaw puzzle."

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.360
The reveal of what Rosebud actually meant,
and the fact that no one in the world of the

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.229
film will ever understand it, comes as a profoundly
emotional shock.

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:05.600
"Rosebud...

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:08.840
It's his sled.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.600
It was his sled from when he was a kid."

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.320
Considering how many times this film has been
parodied, there's no denying its cultural

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.860
imprint, especially in today's nostalgia-obsessed
climate.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.020
"Brain…"

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.590
The shock of a lifetime

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.440
Jackie Chan is no stranger to death-defying
stunts.

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:36.350
The action star claims to have broken nearly
every bone in his body, and that's not hard

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.370
to believe if you've seen any of his movies.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:42.560
But all his jumps, kicks, flips and flails
pale in comparison to his signature stunt

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:43.770
in Police Story.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.030
Known as perhaps the greatest stunt in movie
history, it features Chan jumping off a balcony,

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:52.030
sliding down a pole, and crashing through
live electrical wires and plate glass.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:56.530
The stunt is so iconic that the film shows
it three times in a row, from three different

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:57.530
angles.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:02.590
Chan ended up electrocuting himself, dislocating
his pelvis, and cutting himself on the glass.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:06.880
If this dedication isn't worth a few dozen
viewings, then NOTHING is.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.860
Pool fantasies at Ridgemont High

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:13.290
In the history of cinema, no single moment
has turned more boys into men than Phoebe

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:16.730
Cates' slow-mo strut from the pool in Fast
Times at Ridgemont High.

00:02:16.730 --> 00:02:20.240
It's pretty obvious why this is one of the
most rewatched scenes of all time.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.040
"You know how cute i always thought you were."

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.920
And it's not just about a beautiful girl strutting
around in a bikini; it's funny, too!

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.280
After Brad's fantasy, the audience is then
shown to the cold, hard, embarrassing reality

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:34.489
of the situation.

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:39.240
For anyone who's ever been a teenager in love,
this scene is relatable, iconic, and totally

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.240
rewatchable.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.670
A real trooper

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.230
No one understands better than George Lucas
how difficult it is to make a movie.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:48.569
"But I did have a vision, but my vision was
way beyond what was possible.

00:02:48.569 --> 00:02:50.430
And I did the best I could."

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.260
He did so many things wrong in his original
Star Wars trilogy that he went back in and

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.260
fixed them!

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.910
By fix we mean, of course, jam the movies
so full of special effects that audiences

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:02.710
can barely find any traces of the original
actors or sets in some scenes.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:06.390
But one iconic scene was actually IMPROVED
by Lucas's meddling.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:10.030
Decades of jokes have been made at the expense
of the poor stormtrooper who banged his head

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.250
on the doorway.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:14.709
Lucas even added a "BONK" sound effect to
emphasize the moment for the special edition

00:03:14.709 --> 00:03:15.810
release.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.690
The whisper heard 'round the world

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:22.720
Lost in Translation was one of the most original
and oddly romantic movies to come out in years.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.080
However, it left audiences scratching their
heads with its final scene.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.470
Bill Murray's morose acting legend is saying
goodbye to Scarlett Johansson's lonely newlywed.

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.819
While they share a wonderful friendship, it
is clear that they'll never see each other

00:03:33.819 --> 00:03:34.819
again.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:38.280
Then, Murray leans in close and whispers something
in Johansson's ear.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.480
You can hear that he's whispering, but what
he actually said was too quiet for the mics

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:50.489
to pick up.

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:53.790
There are tons of theories, but it looks like
whatever Murray said to Johansson is staying

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.000
between them.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.640
Even director Sofia Coppola was kept in the
dark!

00:03:57.640 --> 00:04:01.430
That mysterious whisper, and the rest of this
beautiful and poignant film, has kept audiences

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:03.560
coming back for over a decade.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:04.640
A head-spinning ending

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.100
There is nothing more maddening than an ambiguous
ending, and no ambiguous ending was more maddening

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:13.770
than that of Inception, Christopher Nolan's
action/adventure dream heist.

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:18.400
As Leonardo DiCaprio's dream thief Dom Cobb
finally returns home to his children, he sets

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.590
a top spinning on the dining room table.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:24.380
He runs off to greet his children, but the
camera goes back to the top, which was established

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.600
earlier in the film as a tool for Cobb to
tell whether he was awake or still dreaming.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.570
If he was in a dream, the top would spin forever
without falling.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:35.270
Just when the top is about to fall, the movie
cuts to black.

00:04:35.270 --> 00:04:39.120
Nolan has never confirmed any of the extensive
fan theories that have come up.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.550
Perhaps that's the point—trapping you in
an endless loop where you will never know

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.110
how the story ends.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:46.580
Or maybe it's just a cool ending.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:47.840
One morose Munchkin

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.370
Following the Yellow Brick Road sure is fun.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:54.140
That is, until you get old enough to start
noticing what's dangling just through the

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:55.140
trees.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.000
As familiar as The Wizard of Oz is to multiple
generations of fans, one aspect of the film

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.500
remained hidden to all but the most-eagle
eyed viewers.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:07.210
For years, rumors circulated about a Munchkin
hanging in the background while our merry

00:05:07.210 --> 00:05:09.560
band of travelers skipped into the distance.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:12.639
The studio eventually denied this, claiming
that it was was just a bird.

00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:17.360
When the remastered version was released in
1998, the rumor was debunked, as the figure

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:20.120
in the distance was, indeed, just a bird.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.030
Still, stranger things have happened somewhere
over the rainbow...

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.030
Thanks for watching!

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:26.210
